WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.400
If you’re anything like me,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.560
you have lots of ambitious
plans for the future.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.160
I’ve got a whole series of
new books I want to write,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.400
thousands more
videos to script.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.360
But guess what?

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.760
None of us may be able to do
any of the things we want to do

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.240
if we don't have our health.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.480
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.200
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.760
Today we look at new research
about the efficacy of supplements

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.880
in preventing Alzheimer's disease.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.760
Oxidative stress
from free radicals

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:34.840
is the one of the pathways

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.600
implicated in the development
and deterioration

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.440
of Alzheimer's brains,

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.760
given the evidence of excess
oxidative DNA damage on autopsy.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.680
So, what about trying
an antioxidant?

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.400
Vitamin E levels
tend to be lower

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.640
in the bloodstreams of those
with cognitive impairment

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.120
or dementia,
but which came first?

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.160
Maybe Alzheimer's disease
led to poor eating,

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.240
rather than poor eating

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.400
leading to Alzheimer's disease.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.600
Greater vitamin E intake
from foods

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.560
is clearly associated
with a lower risk

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.800
of developing Alzheimer's,

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.440
but greater vitamin E intake
from supplements is not.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.920
But, that didn't stop
researchers

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.720
from including a vitamin E arm

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.640
in the largest primary
prevention study

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.400
of Alzheimer's disease to date.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.200
The Prevention
of Alzheimer's Disease

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.640
by Vitamin E and Selenium trial

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.840
randomized more than
7500 older men

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.400
to take vitamin E, selenium,
both, or neither-just placebos-

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.040
for five years.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.960
To quote from the conclusion:

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.240
"Neither supplement
prevented dementia."

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.400
A separate long-term vitamin E
supplementation trial

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.360
of older women similarly found
no protection

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.280
against cognitive decline.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.560
Neither did vitamin E
appear to benefit those

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.600
with mild cognitive impairment,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.840
but the trials on Alzheimer's
patients themselves

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.080
started to get more interesting.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.200
The first trial,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.960
published in the New England
Journal of Medicine,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.720
randomized more than 150
Alzheimer's patients

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.480
to vitamin E or placebo
to see if vitamin E could slow

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.680
the progression of the disease.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.720
The primary outcome
was the number of days

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.080
until of one of any
of the following happened:

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.640
death, institutionalization,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.040
loss of the ability
to perform basic activities

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.400
of daily living,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.480
or sliding into severe dementia,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.000
where you suffer
a complete loss

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.160
of sense of time or place.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.840
On first analysis,
vitamin E flopped:

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.520
no significant slowing
of progression.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.480
Digging deeper,
the researchers realized

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.720
that despite
the random allotment,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.080
the placebo group
just by chance

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.400
ended up with patients
with milder disease.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.520
After taking that into account,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.520
the difference in outcomes

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.280
between the vitamin E
and placebo groups

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.640
widened substantially.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.920
Now, this kind of
unplanned post-hoc analysis

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.040
is heavily frowned upon,

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.040
but the effect
seemed so sizable

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.840
they went ahead
and published it.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.200
In the placebo group,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.880
the adjusted time to death
or loss of independence

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.520
was 440 days,
but in the vitamin E group,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.040
after the baseline cognition
was normalized,

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:10.480
it was 670 days,
more than seven months later.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.320
However,
given the unorthodox analysis,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:14.800
the scientific world waited

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.080
for the study to be replicated.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:20.000
And here we go:
more than 600 patients

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.960
with mild to moderate
Alzheimer's disease

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.600
were subsequently randomized
to vitamin E supplements,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.800
the drug memantine, both,
or neither (just placebos)

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.760
for two years.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.520
Although there was no slowing
of cognitive decline

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.840
after adjusting
for multiple comparisons,

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.320
there was a slowing
of functional decline,

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.480
in terms of managing
daily activities

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.480
like bathing or dressing.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.640
The vitamin E group
appeared to only suffer

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.840
about 1.5 years
of functional deterioration

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:48.200
in the two years' time,

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.160
resulting in approximately
two hours less caregiver time

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.680
required each day
compared to the drug group.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.240
If those were the only two
clinical vitamin E trials,

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.080
I would give vitamin E
serious consideration

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.040
for those
with Alzheimer's disease.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.360
But, there's a third,
a six-month trial,

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.280
that suggested vitamin E
may be detrimental

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.840
to cognition
in some Alzheimer's patients.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.983
Obviously, more research
needs to be done.

00:04:14.983 --> 00:04:18.586
I'll cover other antioxidants
that have been put to the test,

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.720
including vitamin C,
beta carotene,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.240
Centrum multivitamin,

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.594
and the minerals
zinc and calcium.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.400
Alzheimer's patients have
significantly lower blood levels

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.640
of 8 out of 10 antioxidants
investigated,

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.480
though, of course,
this could just be a consequence

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.440
of demented individuals
eating diets

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.960
poor in fruits and vegetables.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.520
When put to the test,

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.600
the PREADViSE study
found no benefit

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.120
for vitamin E or selenium

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:46.000
in terms of
preventing dementia.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.680
What about other antioxidants?

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.520
In the Harvard Physician's
Health Study,

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.240
nearly 6,000 older men

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.680
were randomized
to beta carotene or placebo.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.720
No difference was found
after three years,

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:58.760
but after 11 years,

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.480
those unknowingly taking
the equivalent

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.760
of 3/4 cup (150 g)
of sweet potato

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.400
worth of beta carotene a day

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.840
had slightly
yet significantly better

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.200
overall cognitive performance.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.040
A similar study of older women

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.960
found no cognitive benefit
over a period of nine years.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.600
Maybe they just didn't wait
long enough?

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.160
Vitamin E and C
were also tested.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.080
Vitamin E similarly flopped
throughout and so did vitamin C,

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.720
up until the last cognitive
assessment at around year 9

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.040
where the vitamin C group
appeared to pull ahead.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.400
However, two trials of
a combination of vitamin C, E,

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.240
and beta carotene together
versus placebo,

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:40.000
one randomizing
1,500 older patients

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.120
for seven years,
and another truly massive one

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.240
randomizing more than
20,000 adults for five years,

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.200
both failed to show
any cognitive benefit

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.320
for the antioxidant cocktail.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:53.200
What about a multivitamin?

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.120
The Physician's Health Study
also tested

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.400
taking the daily
multivitamin/multimineral

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.560
supplement Centrum Silver
for 12 years.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.000
Compared to those instead
randomized to a placebo,

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.200
the Centrum group experienced
no cognitive benefit.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.200
Souvenaid is a nutritional drink

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.320
containing a patented
formulation of nutrients,

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.200
branded as Fortasyn Connect,

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:17.280
specifically designed to help
in Alzheimer's disease.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.120
Unfortunately,
it doesn't work.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:21.960
There have been three
randomized controlled trials

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.920
enrolling more
than a thousand people,

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:26.880
and those taking Souvenaid
were no less likely

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.000
to develop dementia
in the first place,

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.680
and probably it has
little or no effect

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.680
on measures of memory
or other thinking skills

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:34.800
in both those
with mild cognitive impairment

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.080
or Alzheimer's disease.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:41.040
In terms of specific minerals
other than the failed selenium,

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.200
zinc has similarly flopped
to improve overall cognition

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.760
over months or years
of supplementation,

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:49.840
and it's possible
calcium supplements

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.600
could make things worse.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.160
Cross-sectionally,
those taking calcium

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.860
have more "hyperintensity"
brain lesions on MRI

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.120
(shown here in red, denoting
evidence of mini-strokes)

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.440
and longitudinally over time,

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:04.920
women taking
calcium supplements

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:09.080
appear to have twice the odds
of developing dementia.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.120
The Women's Health Initiative
should have been able

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.560
to settle the question
once and for all.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.480
Thousands of older women
were randomized

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.440
to calcium supplements
(plus vitamin D)

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:20.200
or placebo
for about eight years.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.240
Thankfully, there was
no difference in the risk

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:24.600
of developing cognitive
impairment or dementia.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.240
But that's what
was originally found

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.040
for heart attack
and stroke rates

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:30.840
before pre-existing
supplement use

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.360
was taken into account.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.320
See, more than half of the women

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.000
were already taking
personal calcium supplements

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.120
before entering the study

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:41.080
and being randomized
to calcium or placebo.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:44.120
You can imagine how this
could muddy the results.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.360
If you just look at the women

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.920
who weren't
on calcium beforehand,

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.640
starting calcium resulted
in a significant increase

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:53.960
in the risk
of cardiovascular events,

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.800
consistent with a meta-analysis
of calcium trials

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.560
showing a 15 percent increase
in heart attacks or strokes

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:01.640
among those randomized
to calcium supplements

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.320
over placebo.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.560
Unfortunately,
such a reanalysis

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:09.000
has yet to be performed
on the dementia data.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.480
The bottom line is that vitamin
and mineral supplements

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.400
probably aren't going to help
prevent dementia or treat it,

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:19.150
but there are tons
of other things we can do.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:21.960
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:25.400
